# Solution

**Answer: No.** Not every continuous bounded function $u:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ can be uniformly approximated by linear combinations of exponential functions $e^{\lambda t}$ ($\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$).

## Proof

**Step 1: Boundedness forces non-positive exponents.**

Consider a finite linear combination $f(t)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}c_k\,e^{\lambda_k t}$ with $\lambda_k\in\mathbb{R}$. For $f$ to be bounded on $[0,\infty)$, every $\lambda_k$ must satisfy $\lambda_k\le 0$. Indeed, if some $\lambda_k>0$, then $|c_k\,e^{\lambda_k t}|\to\infty$ as $t\to\infty$, and no cancellation by the other (slower-growing or decaying) terms can prevent $|f(t)|\to\infty$.

**Step 2: Every such combination has a limit at infinity.**

For $f(t)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}c_k\,e^{\lambda_k t}$ with all $\lambda_k\le 0$, the terms with $\lambda_k<0$ satisfy $e^{\lambda_k t}\to 0$, while terms with $\lambda_k=0$ contribute the constant $c_k$. Hence

$$\lim_{t\to\infty} f(t) \;=\; \sum_{\{k:\,\lambda_k=0\}} c_k,$$

which exists as a finite real number.

**Step 3: Uniform limits preserve existence of the limit at infinity.**

Suppose $f_n\to u$ uniformly on $[0,\infty)$, and each $f_n$ has a (finite) limit $L_n=\lim_{t\to\infty}f_n(t)$. Then $u$ also has a limit as $t\to\infty$.

*Proof of the claim.* Given $\varepsilon>0$, pick $N$ with $\|f_N-u\|_\infty<\varepsilon/3$. Since $f_N(t)\to L_N$, there exists $T>0$ such that $|f_N(t)-f_N(s)|<\varepsilon/3$ for all $t,s>T$. Then for all $t,s>T$:

$$|u(t)-u(s)|\le |u(t)-f_N(t)|+|f_N(t)-f_N(s)|+|f_N(s)-u(s)|<\varepsilon.$$

Thus $(u(t))_{t>0}$ is Cauchy as $t\to\infty$, so $\lim_{t\to\infty}u(t)$ exists. $\square$

**Step 4: Counterexample.**

Take $u(t)=\sin t$. This is continuous and bounded on $[0,\infty)$, but $\lim_{t\to\infty}\sin t$ does not exist. By Steps 1–3, any function uniformly approximable by linear combinations of real exponentials must possess a limit at infinity. Therefore $\sin t$ **cannot** be uniformly approximated by such linear combinations.

**Conclusion.** The class of continuous bounded functions on $[0,\infty)$ that are uniform limits of linear combinations of exponentials $e^{\lambda t}$ ($\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$) is a proper subclass: it is contained in the set of continuous bounded functions having a limit at infinity. Since there exist continuous bounded functions (e.g., $\sin t$) with no limit at infinity, the answer is **no**.

